From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm cuda: TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_KDA, a KDA mixer site's chain run piece by piece ahead of each piece's o_proj and exchange

A KDA mixer site of a prompt chunk runs its whole chain (kda_prep, kda_step and kda_out: 4.1 ms for 4,096 rows, 34 of the 45 sites)
before the first piece's o_proj, so the first partials exchange starts only after the chain, and the site's side-stream work (four
exchanges, the glue and four shares) then outlasts the 1.9 ms of o_proj fills: in queue85's timeline the main stream is 5.0 ms from
the chain's end to the next router's start, 2.2 ms of it waiting.

TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_KDA=1 runs the chain in ranges of rows instead: cuda_forward_chain.exchange takes the chunk's pieces as cut (a last
piece under 128 rows joined to the one before, cuda_forward_pieces.frontRanges with no minimum), and ahead of the first piece of each
range it launches that range's chain (cuda_kda_block.chainRange: kda_prep, kda_step and kda_out over rows lo..hi, every row-indexed
buffer moved down lo rows by fromRow), then the range's pieces' o_proj and partials exchange as before (exchangePieces split into a
loop and a per-piece function, exchangePiece). The projections still run first over every row, or were run by the previous ffn
site's front. The bits are the same: kda_prep and kda_out work on one row each; kda_step walks its rows in order, carrying the
recurrent state in registers and storing it in fp32 at the end, so a range reading the state the range before it stored is the same
sequence of operations; a range at lo reads the three conv rows before it from a window of projected rows lo - 3 .. lo - 1 (the
layer's own conv_shift kernel, into a scratch window) where the whole chain reads them in place. The state hops between the
committed buffer and a second one, so no range reads the buffer it writes and the layer's old state is untouched until the commit
(cuda_kda_block.hop); cuda_state.make gains that size (the second state buffer and the window, twin_checked 76 to 77). After the
last range the layer commits as the whole chain does: the conv window of the chunk's last three rows and the buffer flip.

Default off: with the switch unset nothing launches differently. The schedule is today's, switch or not, for a chunk under 64
rows (the whole chain takes the fused kernel there), a profiled pass, TF_GLM_L2PF, a shared window's segments and the chunked chain
(cuda_forward_chain.handsChain).

Host test: the switch, the state hops (never a read of what a range writes, the first buffer never written, the last range into the
committed one, for 1 to 16 ranges), the row offsets and the range bookkeeping (cuda_forward_chain.opening: for every chunk size and piece
count, each range opens at its first piece, the ranges come in order and cover the rows once, and each piece lies inside its range)
(cuda_piece_kda_test.zig, listed in cuda.zig's test block). The registry row
and the docs line follow TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_FRONT's. The engine logs the switch at boot.

GPU check: `tf-cuda-test glm-kda-pieces` (zig/tests/cuda/glm_kda_pieces_check.zig, registered in glm_ovn_checks.zig): synthetic rows
through production's own chainRows, commitConv, chainRange, convWindow and hop, seven cases at 11 and 10 heads (4,096 rows in 4 and
8 pieces, 2,048 in 3, 1,326 in 4, 1,160 in 4 with an 8-row last piece, 129 and 300 rows), the chain by ranges against the whole
chain in out, the final state, the saved k, v and decay rows and the conv window, byte for byte (the ranges are the production cut,
cuda_forward.ranges then frontRanges), and two planted controls (a wrong conv window, a wrong start state) that must differ. Not run on a GPU yet.

New here, not a re-seat: written on this series at v1.2.9.

